        "text": "Ultimately, light nodes rely on full nodes to operate. Then there are mining nodes. Technically, mining nodes are either full nodes or light nodes, but these are a subset of nodes that perform the proof of work necessary to secure the network. For cryptocurrencies like Bitcoin that use proof of work consensus mechanisms, a simple computer isn't enough to perform the hashing operations. Because there's so much competition in mining, specialized machines must be used. Examples include GPUs, as you might find in gaming computers, or purpose built devices like application specific integrated circuits. We say that public blockchains are permissionless and anyone can become a node. You might be wondering, with nobody in charge, how can changes be made to the network? In a truly decentralized network, changes can be made with soft forks or hard forks.",
        "start": 640,
        "end": 50610,
